</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We use two sets of benchmarks in our evaluation: a micro-benchmark set and a macro-benchmark set.
The micro-benchmark comes from a prior user study on C to Rust translation, which contains 6 standalone C programs, with an average of 424 lines of code each.
We manually craft at least 60 test cases for each program in the micro benchmark with an average of 89% line coverage, which are used to evaluate the correctness of translations.
We use this small-scale benchmark to demonstrate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>’s ability to adapt to different LLMs and coding agent frameworks, and to perform an ablation study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our main macro-benchmark is a set of
57<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            The full set of BSDCoreUtils contains 69 programs, but we filter out 12 of them from our main results due to three reasons: (1) some utilities are trivial (e.g., <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">true</span>, <span id="footnote2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">yes</span>) (2) certain utilities require special environments (e.g., <span id="footnote2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">stty</span>) or privileges (e.g., <span id="footnote2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">sudo</span>) not available in our experimental sandbox; and (3) certain utilities perform destructive operations (e.g., <span id="footnote2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">rmdir</span>) that may break the testing harness.</span></span></span>
real-world system utility programs (e.g., <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ls</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cp</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xargs</span>) obtained from a software project named BSDCoreUtils. It has an average of 492 lines of code each.
We use this benchmark to evaluate the scalability of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, and compare correctness to our baselines.
These programs do not come with tests or test runners. We preprocessed these programs to add test runners, which can automatically run a set of given tests for each program, compare C and Rust outputs, as well as produce coverage reports. Note that while we provide the test runner, it is infeasible to manually create comprehensive test cases for all programs to evaluate which translation strategy is more correct. Thus, unlike micro-benchmarks, we perform a relative evaluation between <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> and baselines, as described below in the Evaluation Metrics section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To minimize measurement noise and make our experiments automatable, our evaluation focuses on single-threaded C programs that have deterministic behaviors across different executions, which is currently an assumption of our testing framework that both the translator and the discriminator depend on. Table <a href="#S4.T1.st2" title="In Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Experiment Settings ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> summarizes the statistics of two benchmarks,
where we categorize the 57 programs into five categories: process execution control, file/directory management, shell scripting commands, system information access, and textural content processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent Frameworks and Models.</span>
We use Claude Code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025a</a>)</cite> with Claude Sonnet 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025b</a>)</cite> as the main agent framework and LLM for our experiments.
To test if our approach generalizes to other agent frameworks and LLMs, we also performed experiments using Mini-SWE-Agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">SWE-agent, 2025</a>)</cite>, a popular open-source agent framework, with both GPT 5 mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> and Claude 4 Sonnet as the LLM.
We use ‘ClaudeCode’, ‘SWE+Sonnet’, and ‘SWE+GPT5mini’ to represent them in later sections, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison Baselines.</span>
We compare <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> with two non-adversarial baselines, and one adversarial LLM agent baseline.
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Naive baseline:</span> a single agent instructed to translate the C program using only an initial set of seed tests, and does not iterate to add more tests.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coverage baseline:</span> a two-agent setup similar to <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, but the discriminator agent is only instructed to find additional tests that increase the line coverage of the C program, so the new tests do not specifically try to reveal inequivalent behavior.
(3) <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ACToR-NoFuzz</span><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span> <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> but without the fuzzing tool.
Comparing <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> with the first two baselines demonstrates the benefit (if any) of our adversarial design.
Comparing <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> with <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text">ACToR-NoFuzz</span> demonstrates the benefit (if any) of our proposed fuzzing tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>
Our primary evaluation metric to evaluate the correctness of an individual translation is <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pass rate</span>, which is the percentage of tests that pass for a given set of tests.
For our micro-benchmark, we use our manually written test cases to compute pass rates for each benchmark.
For our macro-benchmarks, which do not have manually written tests, we compute pass rates using a set of tests produced by a competing method, and refer to this metric as <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative pass rate</span>. For example, when comparing <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> with the coverage baseline, we compute pass rate for <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> using the tests generated by the coverage baseline, and vice versa.
We use this approach because a given method might get biased towards its own tests (usually a given method passes all of its own tests).
Relative pass rate provides a strong signal for comparing <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> against our baselines. In addition, the test sets used to compute relative pass rate achieve an average of 89.7% line coverage on the micro benchmark and 90.6% line coverage on the macro benchmark, by unioning test cases across translation methods.
This indicates they are a useful signal for overall correctness as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Configuration.</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> and all baselines start with 15 manually crafted, diverse seed tests based on the C code for each program
<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            The seed tests carry intended test format that both translator and discriminator of <span id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> use. This avoids a cold-start for <span id="footnote3.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> as well as all other systems we compare to.</span></span></span>.
For the coverage baseline, <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR-NoFuzz</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, we run 10 iterations of test generation and translation. On each iteration, we generate 3 new tests, resulting in a total of 45 tests per program in the end.
We choose 10 iterations because more iterations usually do not result in improvements in pass rate and limit the incurred financial costs. We generate 3 tests per iteration because larger values lead to a decline in quality of tests, and smaller values have slower convergence.
In considering cost, we allow translator agents and discriminator agents to proceed directly to the next iteration if they are unable to make progress (i.e., generate valid test cases or correct the translation to pass the current tests) after 3 retries.
The prompt for the translator agent is shared among all the translation methods and put in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt Template ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
The prompts for the test generator agent in the coverage baseline and the discriminator of <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> are in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt Template ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Evaluation Result on Micro Benchmark</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03879v1/imgs/three_settings_program_bars.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:532/173;" width="532" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overall correctness (pass rate) achieved by <span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> on micro benchmark across different settings compared with naive baseline, on 3 different agent-model settings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness &amp; Adaptability.</span>
We first test <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> on the micro benchmark with different agents and LLMs.
To analyze the correctness improvement, we compare <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> approach with a <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">naive baseline</span> on pass rate.
The experiment results per program are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Result on Micro Benchmark ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
All translations from <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> and the naive baseline are 100% safe and free of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unsafe</span> sections.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> consistently
improves the translation correctness on all 3 agent-model settings.
With Claude Code, the validation
pass rate rises from an average of 79.3% (one-shot naive baseline) to 92.1% after 10 iterations.
For Mini-SWE-Agent, pass rates increase from 81.9% to 90.7% with Sonnet-4 and from 84.1% to 86.8% with GPT-5 mini on average.
Across agent-LLM settings, the closed-source Claude Code achieves the highest final pass rate.
We therefore use Claude Code + Claude-Sonnet-4 as the default configuration for macro benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Study.</span>
We conduct an ablation to know how the adversarial design in <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> drives correctness gains on the micro-benchmark. We analyze the improvement offered by two design choices: (a) having an <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adversarial discriminator</span> in the workflow to guide the translator, and (b) forcing the discriminator agent to use the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuzzing script</span> we provided.
We compare three variants of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>: coverage-baseline, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text">ACToR-NoFuzz</span>, and the full <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> (with fuzzing).
The results summed across programs at the final iteration are reported in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Result on Micro Benchmark ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Entry <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{row},\text{column})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>row</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>column</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{row},\text{column})</annotation></semantics></math> shows the relative pass rate of the row method on the column method’s tests.
All three methods largely pass their own tests as expected.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;"><img src="2510.03879v1/imgs/method_comparison_heatmap_iter_10.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:274/222;" width="274" height="222" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
The relative comparison among 3 different translation methods on Claude Code.
Entry <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{row},\text{column})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>row</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>column</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{row},\text{column})</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative pass rate of the row’s
translation on the column method’s tests.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On coverage-baseline tests, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR-NoFuzz</span> and the full <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> reach 92% and 88% passing rates, respectively; in the reverse direction, coverage-baseline achieves less than 70% passing rate on tests from either <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> variant.
This indicates that adversarial discriminators produce tests that more effectively expose semantic mismatches and result in more correct translations.
Between <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> variants, the full <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> fails more on its own tests, but attains 82% vs. 75% when cross-tested against <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text">ACToR-NoFuzz</span>. This suggests that the discriminating agent uses the fuzzing script to surface more challenging bugs in the translated code.
We also measure the final test coverage of each method on the C code. The result is shown at the bottom of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Result on Micro Benchmark ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The coverage baseline works as expected and achieves the highest line coverage on the C code than two other <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> variants. However, it is worse in correctness. This further supports the conclusion that adversarial translation is more efficient in improving correctness compared with purely sampling new test cases based on coverage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="70.06" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 70.06" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,70.06) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 64.74 C 0 67.68 2.38 70.06 5.32 70.06 L 544.68 70.06 C 547.62 70.06 550 67.68 550 64.74 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 64.74 C 1.38 66.92 3.15 68.68 5.32 68.68 L 544.68 68.68 C 546.85 68.68 548.62 66.92 548.62 64.74 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 5.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.78em;--ltx-fo-height:4.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="59.42" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.42)" width="536.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.78em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">On our micro benchmark, the full <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> system substantially improves translation correctness compared with the naive baseline. The adversarial discriminator is crucial to improving correctness, and the fuzzing tool further strengthens the discovery of mismatches.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03879v1/imgs/cross_val_res_v2.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:543/256;" width="543" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
The relative pass rate when cross-comparing <span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> and coverage baseline (Cov-Base) on Claude Code at iteration 10. For each program, the left bar shows evaluating the translation from <span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> on tests generated in coverage-baseline; the right bar is evaluating the translation from coverage-baseline on <span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> tests. The length of each program in LoC is presented next to the program name.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluation on Macro Benchmark</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> results with the coverage baseline to examine the improvement achieved in correctness, using the relative pass rate metric.
We use the Claude Code agent and Claude-Sonnet-4 for this experiment.
In considering the necessary system calls used in the translated code, we discourage the use of unsafe by instructing in prompts, but do not enforce full safe Rust in the middle of the translation loop.
We enforce 100% safe Rust at the end of the translation in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, as an additional simple post-processing agent call.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cross-comparison result at the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> iteration<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            On 3 programs ‘sync‘, ‘users‘, and ‘mknod‘, both translation methods abort before 10 iterations after multiple reruns. We therefore compare them on the maximum available iteration.</span></span></span> is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Result on Micro Benchmark ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The left bar for each program is the relative pass rate of testing <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>’s translation on the test cases from the coverage baseline. The right bar shows the reverse: relative pass rate of the coverage baseline against <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>’s test cases.
We see that <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> outperforms the coverage baseline on 55/57 programs on the relative pass rate.
<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> gets 100% relative pass rate on 26 programs when running on the tests from the coverage baseline, while the coverage baseline can only get full pass on 1 program.
On 57 programs, <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> achieves an average 93.9% relative pass rate, 18.9% higher than the 75.0% relative pass rate obtained by the coverage baseline.
The cross-comparison confirms that adversarial agentic design in <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> plays a significant role in improving the translation correctness.
In an absolute (non-relative) sense of correctness, by taking the union of the test cases from both <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> and the coverage baseline, we get a test set of an average 90.6% coverage.
<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> achieves a 95.3% pass rate on it.
This demonstrates the utility of <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> on translating real-world projects with high correctness.
We further analyze the only benchmark program, specifically the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E3E6E8;">pr</span> program, for which <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> has lower correctness than the coverage baseline in the Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Case Study ‣ Adversarial Agent Collaborationfor C to Rust Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="56.15" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 56.15" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,56.15) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 50.83 C 0 53.77 2.38 56.15 5.32 56.15 L 544.68 56.15 C 547.62 56.15 550 53.77 550 50.83 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 50.83 C 1.38 53 3.15 54.77 5.32 54.77 L 544.68 54.77 C 546.85 54.77 548.62 53 548.62 50.83 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.7 8.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.78em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="536.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.78em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ACToR</span><span id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> automatically translates the BSDCoreUtil benchmark with high correctness, outperforming the coverage baseline on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">57</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57</annotation></semantics></math> programs and achieving an average of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="93.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">93.9</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">93.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative pass rate.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, an agent-based automatic translation approach that aims for functionally correct C to Rust translations.
Our method introduces a collaborative pipeline in which two agents iteratively refine the translations.
At each iteration, the discriminant agents detect the semantic mismatches between the source code and the translated program, and report them as failing test cases.
The translator agents then improve the translation to pass the current test set.
Our experiments demonstrate the power of <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>’s adversarial agentic setup to produce translations with high correctness, surpassing the quality of translation as compared to non-adversarial setups.
A noteworthy advantage that <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> brings to the translation landscape is the potential for large-scale translation with minimal or no human intervention, making <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span> the first approach of this kind.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The artifact containing detailed instructions, benchmark programs, and evaluation scripts will be released soon.
Section 3 describes the algorithmic design of <span id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">ACToR</span>, including assumptions and iteration parameters, while Section 4 outlines our evaluation methodology and benchmark datasets. Appendix B includes the prompt template used for the translator and discriminator agents. Together, these resources enable independent verification of both our experimental setup and results.</p>
</div>
</section>
